          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30132v1",
          "title": "LongZero: Enhancing Long-Context Reasoning in LLMs from Zero Data",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:59:14Z",
          "authors": [
            "Zhennan Wan",
            "Jianfei Chen"
          ],
          "summary": "Reinforcement learning (RL) is an effective way to strengthen the long-context reasoning of large language models (LLMs), but it needs training data that are long, diverse, challenging, and verifiable. Human annotation of such data is expensive, naturally occurring data are too scarce to sustain RL, and existing synthesis methods depend on external corpora or stronger teacher models, which become harder to obtain as the trained models get stronger. Recently, RL from zero data, in which a model l"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30128v1",
          "title": "Think Harder Where It Matters: Item-Level Adaptive Recursion for Sequential Recommendation",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:58:06Z",
          "authors": [
            "Zhanshuo Cao",
            "Jiakai Tang",
            "Yuqi Zhou",
            "Sunhao Dai",
            "Jun Xu"
          ],
          "summary": "Existing sequential recommenders usually allocate a fixed computational budget to every item in a user's interaction history, regardless of the item's contribution to the final prediction. We challenge this de facto design with two diagnostic findings. (1) Non-uniform computational need: items within the same sequence vary drastically in how much computation they require. Most converge after shallow processing while a critical minority only stabilizes at full depth. (2) Suboptimality of fixed al"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30125v1",
          "title": "PhysiCalWorld: In-Context Physics Calibration for World Models",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:57:59Z",
          "authors": [
            "Yunhao Luo",
            "Wentao He",
            "Yinpei Dai",
            "Hyelin Nam",
            "Hongze Fu",
            "Joyce Chai",
            "Nima Fazeli",
            "Jeong Joon Park"
          ],
          "summary": "Existing world models typically perceive a scene through current visual frame or a narrow history window. However, these observations may not reveal the physics properties that determine transition dynamics, such as an object's mass distribution, joint damping, or surface friction. Without calibrating these physics factors for world models, predictions tend to regress toward an empirical prior and induce severe drift in novel test scenes. To this end, we propose PhysiCalWorld, which calibrates a"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30122v1",
          "title": "A Quasipolynomial Algorithm for $\\ell_\\infty$ Contractions",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:57:19Z",
          "authors": [
            "Ashkan Soleymani",
            "Gabriele Farina"
          ],
          "summary": "We give an algorithm for computing an $\\eps$-approximate fixed point of any $d$-dimensional nonexpansion with respect to the $\\ell_\\infty$ norm in time $\\log(1/\\eps)^{O(\\log^2 d)}$. At its core, our method uses a boosting argument to geometrically shrink a search region. Among other consequences, our algorithm implies that simple stochastic games can be solved in quasipolynomial time."
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30119v1",
          "title": "Self-normalized Bernstein Inequalities for Regenerative Processes and Applications to Learning Without Independence",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:56:37Z",
          "authors": [
            "Imon Banerjee",
            "Pierre Alquier"
          ],
          "summary": "We prove a self-normalized Bernstein inequality for bounded functions of a regenerative process with independent, identically distributed marked cycles of finite mean length. The bound uses observed cycle lengths and retains explicit terms for truncation and the cycle ending after the observation horizon. Selecting a tuning parameter from a finite deterministic collection gives a version of Bernstein inequality where all terms are empirical but one, related to the tails of the regeneration times"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30118v1",
          "title": "The role of locality and weight sharing in convolutional neural networks",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:56:22Z",
          "authors": [
            "Francesco Mori",
            "Melanie Weber"
          ],
          "summary": "The success of convolutional neural networks is often attributed to locality and weight sharing. However, how these two properties separately shape learning, and how their effects depend on the spatial correlations of natural images, remain poorly understood. We address these questions in a teacher-student model with a convolutional teacher acting on $P$ input positions with correlation $c$ between positions. We compare convolutional and locally connected students trained by online stochastic gr"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30116v1",
          "title": "Environment Duels: A Microcosm of LLM Co-Evolution",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:55:31Z",
          "authors": [
            "Shibo Jin",
            "Zhiqiu Xu",
            "Yiling Zou",
            "Shangyu Gong",
            "Mayur Naik"
          ],
          "summary": "Through a tangle of real-world interactions, from synthetic training pipelines to data shared on the open web, frontier LLMs absorb what their predecessors and competitors create. How well does each model play its role in this ecosystem, as a creator and as a solver? We introduce Environment Duels, a dynamic agentic benchmark that evaluates both capabilities on interactive environments. Each model writes environments, each with a privileged hint, aiming for tasks that capable solvers fail withou"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30114v1",
          "title": "The End of Consensus Research?",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:55:19Z",
          "authors": [
            "Clément Burgelin",
            "Rachid Guerraoui",
            "Zicong Hong",
            "Yinan Hu",
            "Anne-Marie Kermarrec",
            "Rafael Pires",
            "Rasmus Moorits Veski",
            "Martijn de Vos"
          ],
          "summary": "Consensus is a fundamental problem in distributed computing and at the core of state machine replication (SMR). Despite decades of active human research, discovering practical low-latency solutions to the problem is still open, as it requires combining protocol knowledge, correctness reasoning, implementation, and experimental evaluation. We report on how AI helped us discover A-greement, a new consensus protocol for geo-replicated state machines that significantly outperforms state-of-the-art s"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30113v1",
          "title": "Quant-Arena: Benchmarking LLM Agents for Quantitative Trading Research",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:54:01Z",
          "authors": [
            "Houde Qian",
            "Zheng Jiang",
            "Wenyi Li",
            "Chenxi Xu",
            "Xinjie Lin",
            "Yuzhao Peng",
            "Ruoyu Han",
            "Youjie Zheng",
            "Kaisen Yang",
            "Qinhuai Na",
            "You Fu",
            "Calvin Xiao"
          ],
          "summary": "Quantitative research is an iterative loop: a researcher proposes a market hypothesis, implements it as an executable signal, tests it on historical data, and decides what to keep. Existing benchmarks score formulas, code, or individual trading decisions, leaving open whether large language models (LLMs) can complete this loop and produce strategies that work beyond the research period. We introduce Quant-Arena, a benchmark and public leaderboard for end-to-end quantitative research on real A-sh"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30109v1",
          "title": "Minimax Optimality of the NPMLE for Gaussian Mixtures in Hellinger Distance",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:53:10Z",
          "authors": [
            "Shayan Hundrieser",
            "Tudor Manole"
          ],
          "summary": "We analyze the problem of estimating univariate Gaussian mixture densities using the nonparametric maximum likelihood estimator (NPMLE) based on $n$ independent and identically distributed samples. We show that the unconstrained NPMLE achieves the minimax optimal rate $\\log(n)/n$ in squared Hellinger distance, when the true mixing measure is sub-Gaussian. In addition, we show that when the true mixing measure has compact support, the NMPLE constrained to a compact interval also achieves the mini"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30108v1",
          "title": "Multimodal Embodied Agents: A Unified Capability-centric Perspective from Computer-Use to Robot-Use",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:52:48Z",
          "authors": [
            "Yanzhe Chen",
            "Ziyi Yang",
            "Jifeng Zhu",
            "Qiming Huang",
            "Ruihe An",
            "Peiyao Xu",
            "Hesen Yang",
            "Runda Liu",
            "Chang Gong",
            "Zhijun Cao",
            "Zechen Bai",
            "Wenzheng Zeng",
            "Yiqi Lin",
            "Guoqiang Liang",
            "Kevin Yuchen Ma",
            "Kevin Qinghong Lin",
            "Mike Zheng Shou"
          ],
          "summary": "Multimodal agents (MMAs) sustain interaction through reasoning, memory, tools, and feedback, most visibly as computer-use agents, while robotic systems (RSs) couple sensing and actuation under physical dynamics. We define multimodal embodied agents (MMEAs) as goal-directed systems that couple multimodal task reasoning with physical action and revise decisions from the resulting feedback. This raises a central question: what changes when a multimodal agent moves from computer-use to robot-use? We"
        },
        {
          "id": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30107v1",
          "title": "Velocity Ambiguity Profiles: Time-Resolved Bayes-Risk Diagnostics for Flow Matching",
          "published": "2026-10-21T17:52:40Z",
          "authors": [
            "Yunpeng Mei",
            "Xiaowen Zhu",
            "Chenyu Wang",
            "Chenbo Xin",
            "Hongjie Cao",
            "Jiamin Wang",
            "Jie Chen",
            "Gang Wang"
          ],
          "summary": "Flow matching trains a continuum of velocity regression problems but usually reports a loss averaged over interpolation time, obscuring which regions are limited by intrinsic ambiguity and which remain data-limited. We introduce the Velocity Ambiguity Profile (VAP), $\\mathcal{A}_v(t)$, the time-local Bayes-risk floor of velocity prediction, and decompose time-resolved loss as $L_N(t)=\\mathcal{A}_v(t)+R(t;N)$, where only the excess term $R(t;N)$ is reducible by more data or a different estimator."
        }
      ]
    }
